  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat Advisory Thursday for mainly the piedmont.\n\nTemperatures are going to stay well above normal for early \nSeptember again Thursday. The only thing Thursday that could \nkeep it from getting as hot as today would be any convective \nblowoff from upstream deep convection but overall the model \nupper winds are going to be more out of the west while the \nstorms move from north-northwest to the south-southeast across \nnorthern VA tomorrow.\n\nDespite some areas not reaching quite 105, given its September\nand with outdoor/school activities feel keep the heat advisory \nis warranted.\n\nThings start to trend down in temps with increasing chances for\nshowers/storms into the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Multiple chance of showers and thunderstorms\nThursday through this weekend. Some strong/severe especially\nFriday.\n\nCannot rule out a few storms around the Buckingham to South\nBoston area this evening but models overall take the better\ncluster of storms over to the east toward the coast.\n\nShould see a slight uptick in chances tomorrow though the upper\nridge still holds on somewhat but heights slightly fall across\nVA.\n\nAt least widely scattered storms of strong/severe variety are\npossible...along/north of I-64 and east of the Blue Ridge. But\nsimilar to Wednesday, outflow may allow storms to propagate\nfarther south with potential to reach highway 460 before loss of\ndaytime heating. \n\nFrom SPC - Despite weakening mid-level lapse rates with \neastward extent, daytime heating combined with dewpoints largely\nin the low/mid 70s will contribute to moderate to strong \ninstability by afternoon along and south of the effective \nsurface front with MLCAPE approaching 2500-3500 J/kg. The \nfrontal zone will coincide with a belt of 40-45 kt west-\nnorthwest mid-level flow, yielding sufficient vertical shear for\nsupercells and organized line segments and/or bowing \nstructures. The primary hazard will be damaging wind gusts, \nthough isolated occurrences of large hail are possible with any \nsupercells that may evolve.\n\nAppears this may be more likely east and north of our area\nespecially hail chances given warmer ridge. Still damaging winds\nare a concern given the available energy from the heat.\n\nThe cold front's passage will provide another round of showers and \nthunderstorms on Saturday into Sunday, though thunder chances\nshould drop with more stable air moving in behind the front. \n\n\nWe should dry out early next week but heat builds again by\nmiddle of next week with another 5h ridge.",
